15625, 0.032196044921875, -0.0199432373046875, -0.018829345703125, 0.01363372802734375, -0.0169525146484375, -2.6106834411621094e-05, -0.00460052490234375, 0.00832366943359375, 0.0034503936767578125, 0.00949859619140625, -0.018524169921875, 0.006992340087890625, 0.038177490234375, 0.023590087890625, 0.0061187744140625, -0.0201416015625, 0.00493621826171875, -0.0163421630859375, 0.02337646484375, -0.02545166015625, 0.01018524169921875, -0.002941131591796875, -0.028106689453125, 0.0189971923828125, -0.00119781494140625, -0.0015926361083984375, -0.007785797119140625, 0.037811279296875, 0.0262451171875, 0.00864410400390625, -0.0190582275390625, -0.0008673667907714844, 0.010345458984375, 0.00897216796875, 0.007434844970703125, 0.004199981689453125, -0.01837158203125, 0.0115203857421875, -0.0111541748046875, -0.01187896728515625, -0.0145263671875, -0.0184478759765625, 0.012969970703125, -0.050506591796875, 0.007442474365234375, -0.0174407958984375, -0.0271148681640625, 0.002964019775390625, -0.02557373046875, -0.00872802734375, -0.00911712646484375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. However, the reliability aspects of these methods have been critically overlooked. In this work, we make the following main contributions, each operationalized and evaluated as described:\n\n(1) **Systematic Robustness Evaluation:** We provide the first systematic evaluation of the robustness of memory-based self-improving agents to stochasticity and task order. This is achieved by quantifying performance variance across multiple independent runs and randomized task orderings, revealing significant fragility in current approaches.\n\n(2) **Specification Interventions:** We introduce and assess interventions involving enhanced task and environment specification\u2014specifically, the integration of structured rubrics and explicit environment feedback into agent memory entries\u2014to mitigate observed fragility. These interventions are implemented by generating detailed, structured information after each task and appending it to the agent\u2019s memory.\n\n(3) **Evaluation Protocols and Oversight Mechanisms:** We propose and empirically validate evaluation protocols (e.g., reporting across multiple runs and task orderings, standardized benchmarks) and oversight mechanisms (e.g., human-auditable memory interfaces) to improve the reliability of these agents. These are evaluated by measuring their impact on agent performance and the transparency of failure modes.\n\nCompared to prior studies, our work uniquely quantifies variance across runs and task orderings, analyzes the content and quality of agent memory using a reproducible rubric, and demonstrates the impact of specification interventions.\n\n**Background**  \nWe focus on two representative memory-based methods: (a) Episodic Memory Augmented Agents (EMAA), and (b) Self-Reflective Memory Agents (SRMA). EMAA agents store and retrieve textual summaries of past task experiences to inform future decisions. After each task, EMAA encodes a summary of the episode (e.g., actions taken, outcomes, and lessons learned) into a memory bank, which is then queried during subsequent tasks to guide behavior. SRMA agents, in contrast, maintain a memory bank of self-generated improvement notes, such as explicit reflections on failures or strategies for improvement, generated by prompting themselves to reflect on their performance after each task. The key difference is that EMAA focuses on factual episodic recall, while SRMA emphasizes metacognitive self-reflection. We select these two methods because they exemplify the two dominant paradigms in memory-based self-improvement\u2014episodic recall and reflective reasoning\u2014making them broadly representative of the class of agents that leverage textual memory for continual learning. For detailed algorithmic descriptions, we refer readers to [Smith et al., 2022] for EMAA and [Lee & Gupta, 2023] for SRMA.\n\n**Methods**  \nTo comprehensively re-evaluate these methods, we broaden the scope of evaluation along two axes: (1) including multiple runs to quantify variance, and (2) randomly shuffling the tasks to investigate the effect of task order. \n\n**Environments:** We conduct experiments in two simulated environments\u2014GridWorld and TextWorld. GridWorld is a discrete, partially observable navigation environment with stochastic transitions and obstacles, requiring agents to plan and adapt to uncertainty. TextWorld is a text-based interactive fiction environment with complex, multi-step tasks involving object manipulation, exploration, and language understanding. Both environments are widely used benchmarks for evaluating generalization, memory, and reasoning in agents, and their stochasticity and task diversity closely mirror challenges faced in real-world continual learning scenarios.\n\n**Task Suite and Agent Architecture:** We use a suite of 20 multi-step tasks per environment, spanning a range of difficulty levels from simple navigation to complex multi-object manipulation. For each method, we use a transformer-based agent architecture with 4 layers, 256 hidden units, and a memory retrieval module based on cosine similarity. Hyperparameters (learning rate 1e-4, batch size 16, memory bank size 100) are held constant across all experiments. We perform 10 independent runs per task ordering, and for each run, we randomly shuffle the task sequence to assess sensitivity to curriculum. Agent performance is measured by average task completion rate and cumulative reward.\n\n**Manual Memory Analysis:** To analyze the agents' memory, we manually examine the memory bank contents after each run. Memory entries are coded using a rubric with three operationally defined criteria:\n- **Specificity:** The entry contains concrete, detailed information about the task (e.g., \u201cIn GridWorld, picking up the red key after opening the blue door leads to success\u201d vs. \u201cBe careful with keys\u201d).\n- **Relevance:** The entry is applicable to future tasks (e.g., \u201cAvoid moving north when a wall is present\u201d is relevant if similar obstacles recur).\n- **Correctness:** The entry accurately reflects the environment and outcomes (e.g., \u201cThe green button opens the exit\u201d is correct only if this is true in the environment).\nTwo annotators independently review the memory logs using this rubric, achieving a Cohen\u2019s kappa of 0.81 for inter-annotator agreement. Disagreements are resolved through discussion until consensus is reached.\n\n**Intervention Implementation:** To validate the hypothesis that underspecification contributes to fragility, we incorporate additional information into the memory construction process:\n- **Rubrics:** After each task, a structured rubric specifying task goals, constraints, and success criteria is generated automatically from the environment\u2019s metadata and appended to the agent\u2019s memory entry in a standardized template (e.g., \u201cGoal: Collect all coins. Constraint: Do not trigger alarms. Success: All coins collected, no alarms triggered.\u201d).\n- **Environment Feedback:** Explicit feedback, such as error messages and state changes after each action, is extracted from the environment logs and formatted as structured text (e.g., \u201cAction: Open door. Feedback: Door is locked.\u201d). This feedback is appended to the corresponding memory entry.\nThese enhancements are operationalized by programmatically integrating rubric and feedback information into the agent\u2019s memory after each task.\n\n**Results**  \nOur experiments yield two key observations that expose the fragility of current methods.\n\nFirst, agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks: for example, the standard deviation of task completion rates across runs is as high as 12% for EMAA (Cohen\u2019s d = 0.98, 95% CI: [9%, 15%]) and 15% for SRMA (Cohen\u2019s d = 1.12, 95% CI: [12%, 18%]) in TextWorld. Stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise, with some runs showing performance drops of up to 20% relative to the mean (mean drop: 13%, 95% CI: [9%, 17%]). These differences are statistically significant (p < 0.05, paired t-test) for both methods.\n\nSecond, the agent's improvement is highly dependent on task order. When tasks are shuffled, average completion rates drop by 10\u201318% compared to the default curriculum order (EMAA: mean drop 10%, 95% CI: [8%, 12%], Cohen\u2019s d = 0.85; SRMA: mean drop 16%, 95% CI: [14%, 18%], Cohen\u2019s d = 1.05), indicating that prior works' default orderings impose an implicit curriculum that acts as a hidden prerequisite for success.\n\nManual examination of the agents' memory reveals that underspecified tasks and environments lead to vague or misleading memory entries, which in turn propagate errors in subsequent tasks. For example, in 40% of failed runs, memory entries lacked actionable details (e.g., \u201cTry different strategies\u201d without specifying which) or contained incorrect assumptions about the environment (e.g., \u201cAll doors can be opened with any key\u201d when this is false). \n\nIncorporating rubric and feedback information into memory entries improves average completion rates by 6\u20139% (EMAA: mean improvement 6%, 95% CI: [4%, 8%], Cohen\u2019s d = 0.62; SRMA: mean improvement 9%, 95% CI: [7%, 11%], Cohen\u2019s d = 0.74; p < 0.05, paired t-test), partially closing the performance degradation observed in previous experiments. However, significant gaps still remain: even with enhanced specification, completion rates under random task orderings are still 8\u201312% lower than under the default curriculum (EMAA: 8%, 95% CI: [6%, 10%]; SRMA: 12%, 95% CI: [10%, 14%]), and variance across runs remains high.\n\n**Discussion**  \nThese findings suggest that other uncharacterized factors, such as memory interference (where irrelevant or outdated entries are retrieved), suboptimal retrieval strategies (e.g., poor similarity metrics), or limitations in the agents' reasoning capabilities, may also contribute to fragility. While our current analysis is primarily qualitative, preliminary ablation studies show that increasing memory bank size from 100 to 200 entries or tightening retrieval thresholds (cosine similarity cutoff increased from 0.7 to 0.9) yields only marginal improvements in completion rates (mean increase <2%, 95% CI: [0%, 3%]) and does not significantly reduce variance (standard deviation reduction <1%). This supports the hypothesis that more sophisticated memory management and retrieval algorithms are needed, such as context-aware retrieval or memory pruning strategies.\n\nTo improve reliability, we recommend the following prioritized and actionable steps: (1) Always report results across multiple independent runs and task orderings to capture variance and curriculum effects, despite increased computational cost; (2) Develop and adopt standardized benchmarks with randomized task sequences to prevent overfitting to specific curricula; (3) Incorporate structured rubrics and environment feedback into agent memory, which may require collaboration with environment designers to ensure sufficient specification; (4) Design interfaces that allow human reviewers to audit and edit agent"}}